it's great to be joined today by Terry  Cogan who's professor of law at the SJ  Quinney College of Law at the University  of Utah  you know this topic of bathrooms has  really come to the forefront of national  media mostly through discussions of  so-called transgender bathroom bills and  one of the arguments that we often hear  from those who are against laws that  allow individuals to use or rather those  who want individuals to be forced to use  the bathroom that matches their birth  genitals often argue that it's  completely natural obvious and has been  the way things are done forever that  bathrooms in public are segregated by  genitals that's not actually true is it  that's absolutely not true though you're  right that most people hold that  intuition that it makes perfect sense  that because there are two different  sexes and because people need privacy  that it's the natural way to separate  public restrooms in fact that is not how  the first laws in the United States came  about so talk to us for people who don't  know the backstory  when did sex segregated public restrooms  come about and people may have no idea  that this really does connect to the  industrial revolution in great part  absolutely so the first laws in the  United States  that required sex segregated restrooms  were adopted at the end of the 19th  century and they applied to factories  and other workplaces the first law was  in Massachusetts in 1887 New York  adopted a similar law the next year and  by 1920 over 40 states had adopted laws  requiring the factory restrooms be  separated by sex and I was interested  now several years ago in what led to  these laws being adopted and what I  discovered is that the  origin of these laws actually dates back  to the beginning of the 19th century and  the so called separate spheres ideology  and here's what that's all about until  the end of the 18th century the economy  of the United States what ran largely  out of the home men and women would not  only raise their families in the home  but through trades and crafts create  goods that would then be sold out of the  home as a result of the Industrial  Revolution manufacturing became  centralized in factories in the United  States and what started happening was  that men left the home to work in these  factories whereas women stayed in the  home to take care of their families and  there are roads as I suggested a vision  called the separate spheres ideology  which suggested that the proper sphere  for men was in the public realm in  factories as political leaders and the  proper sphere for women was in the home  watching the heart fires and raising  their kids this ideology was broadly  accepted culturally in the United States  beginning in the early 19th century the  problem was that shortly after the  beginning of the century women began  leaving the home to work in factories in  1822 when the textile factories for  example in Lowell Massachusetts were up  and running a large number of the  operatives in that fact in those  factories were women so there is this  cultural ideology that women should stay  at home and the reality of the evolving  nineteenth century is that women are  leaving the home in droves and you end  up with the following situation every  time a woman left the home to enter the  public realm it created  a kind of social cultural anxiety and in  response to that there were two  reactions by the mid 19th century the  first reaction was that states began  adopting protected labor legislation  that applied to women and children  legislation that limited the number of  hours that women could work legislation  that limited certain dangerous jobs only  to men a women were forbidden from those  jobs but in addition to this legislation  a very interesting architectural  response began happening in the United  States where there were separate public  separate spaces architectural spaces in  buildings created for women so for  example in public libraries early in the  nineteenth century women weren't allowed  in public libraries but once they were  allowed in such libraries beginning in  the 1840s you discover that separate  women's meeting rooms were created you  discovered that in hotels in photography  studios that would be separate in  department stores separate salons for  women and the idea was women are  inherently vulnerable when they enter  the public realm and therefore they need  a protected space when they enter the  public realm  so Terry if I may the it sounds like  what you're sort of saying is as we  often hear from social conservatives in  other areas what they want to present as  sort of the way things have been for as  far back as we can figure it out in  human society was actually something  sort of acutely established some time as  far back as the 19th century as you're  saying or some times as recently as the  1950s but at some point in the last  several hundred years the well be the  1850s  1850s I'm sorry I might have well no but  sometimes it seems like the  Conservatives want to go to the 1950s  specifically so that would be sort of  not with this particular issue but I was  sort of setting about right yeah that's  right that there is a sense that the  establishment of a custom as you  suggested earlier was simply natural the  way things should be and laws simply  reflect that natural state of things and  my research has shown that that's just  wrong so just to finish the story yeah  by the 1880s this notion of creating  exclusive spaces for women in public  began being applied to the factory and  the factory factories in the late 19th  century were dangerous places there is  no doubt about that  moreover given the rise of the Victorian  era men and women would work next to  each other in factories are on assembly  lines which caused great concern so the  solution to protect women and factories  was to create a kind of boom like Haven  in the public realm in the guise of a  separate public restroom there are also  laws that required separate dressing  rooms and there were laws that required  resting rooms for women all aimed at  protecting women in this dangerous  public realm what do we know if we go  further back and this goes beyond the  United States but maybe going back to  Europe in terms of bathroom spaces or  bathroom like spaces of course predating  modern plumbing or anything like it what  do we know about the organization of  these spaces with regard to gender okay  so realistically the possibility of a  multi-user  bathroom did not come about until the  technology of public works of sewer  systems allowed there to be indoor  plumbing  where the effluence was taken away from  buildings through sewers right and that  did not come about the first well-known  public bathrooms were at the Great  Exhibition in London in 18-49 in the  United States the technology did not  reach a stage public works were not  developed enough until the mid 1870s  that would even allow a multi-user  bathroom before then you have basically  separate single user privies outhouses  whatever  once in a while you would find that that  would be two of these privies next to  each other one labeled men one labeled  women but often that would be a single  Privy used by both sexes but what is  important is they were all single user  so the modern tension over bathrooms  only arises because of the possibility  of a multi-user bathroom and in the  United States that did not happen until  roughly the 1870s absolutely fascinating  and this is really really important to  keep in mind when we talk about and  evaluate arguments made around what  bathroom laws should be today it's  really important to have an  understanding of how things actually  were previously in human society we've  been speaking to Terry Cogan professor  of law at the SJ Quinney College of Law  at the University of Utah so great to  talk to you I really appreciate it David  it was good talking to you
 

